# 24. THESAURUS LOGIC AND ETYMOLOGICAL REASONING

## 24.1 Thesaurus Logic

SAGE maintains structured conceptual relationships among programming terminology.

It identifies:

- Exact identities.
- Declared aliases.
- Synonyms.
- Antonyms.
- Broader concepts.
- Narrower concepts.
- Related operations.
- Derived terminology.
- Contextual similarities.
- Incompatible concepts.

Conceptual relationships improve diagnostics, navigation, search and candidate generation.

They do not silently modify name binding.

## 24.2 Etymological knowledge

Etymological reasoning connects terminology to origins, morphology, conceptual history and program-specific definitions.

A conceptual relationship graph is distinguished from a mathematical lattice.

Shared word origins do not establish type equality, inheritance or program equivalence.

## 24.3 Native implementation

The conceptual engine uses native deterministic graph algorithms, indexed lookup, canonical identities and bounded traversal.

Datasets are versioned, hashed and provenance-aware.

Authoritative compilation remains independent of external linguistic services.

